<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudan National Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, opened in 1971, is the largest museum in Sudan. Its collections span Sudanese history from prehistory through the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> and Kush to the Christian Nubian kingdoms, including wall paintings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> cathedral. Its garden holds temples rescued from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> before the flooding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudan-national-museum.jpg" alt="Sudan National Museum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudan_National_Museum_(8625532907).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The museum was housed in a two-storey building constructed in 1955 on Nile Avenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>'s al-Mugran area, close to the confluence of the White and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>. A major spur to its creation was the mass of artefacts relocated during the International Campaign to Save the Monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> of 1960–1980. Before 2023 it held an estimated 100,000 objects, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> to the Islamic period, shown in a main hall, a gallery on the first floor, an open-air museum in the garden and a monumental alley outside.</p><p>A 4-metre granite statue of the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> faced the entrance; it had been broken when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> in 591 BCE, buried by Kushite priests, and excavated by George Reisner in 1916. The first floor held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> wall paintings, dated between the 9th and the 13th century, and Greek and Coptic epitaphs of Christian Nubia catalogued by Adam Lajtar and Jacques Van der Vliet. In the garden the relocated temples of Aksha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a> and others stood around an artificial stream symbolising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The museum was the scene of heavy fighting in the Sudanese civil war between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), which began in April 2023, and the RSF took it over on 2 June 2023. Most exhibits, among them mummies dating to 2500 BCE, were destroyed, looted or badly damaged. Ikhlas Abdel Latif, head of museums at the National Corporation for Antiquities and Museums (NCAM), said that items had been taken by truck to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a> and border areas, and reports said some were sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>.</p><p>After the RSF was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> in March 2025, the director of NCAM, Ghalia Garelnabi, accused it of destroying 90% of the museum's holdings, looting its archaeological gold collection and deliberately smashing other artifacts. According to The Guardian almost all of the 100,000 objects were looted, leaving only very heavy pieces such as monumental statues and rebuilt temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>With the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a>, the French Archaeological Unit for Sudanese Antiquities (SFDAS) helped the museum create a virtual site to display the lost artefacts and recreate its exhibits. At the end of 2022 The Guardian had reported on a new generation of Sudanese archaeologists, many of them young women trained at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, working for NCAM alongside foreign-led research.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Museum of Sudan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a Polish mission led by Kazimierz Michałowski before the area was flooded by Lake Nasser in the 1960s, are now in the National Museum in Warsaw and the <b>Sudan National Museum</b> in Khartoum and show bishops, kings and saints in a style that blends Byzantine and local traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
